1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,240
Two Tigers against 22 Soviet tanks, and he knocked out 15 of them himself.

2
00:00:05,380 --> 00:00:09,320
He was wounded seven times in the war, and yet he survived.

3
00:00:09,540 --> 00:00:11,440
His name was Johannes Bölter.

4
00:00:11,480 --> 00:00:13,540
He was born in 1915.

5
00:00:13,640 --> 00:00:17,960
As early as 1941 he was wounded on the Eastern Front outside Leningrad.

6
00:00:18,040 --> 00:00:22,620
Later he joined Heavy Tank Battalion 502 and was given a Tiger.

7
00:00:22,640 --> 00:00:32,380
On 7 April 1944, south of Pskov, he and a second Tiger destroyed 22 Soviet tanks, 15 of them by himself.

8
00:00:32,700 --> 00:00:34,700
For this he received the Knight's Cross.

9
00:00:34,740 --> 00:00:39,020
In September 1944, as a lieutenant, he was awarded the Oak Leaves.

10
00:00:39,040 --> 00:00:42,360
A month earlier he had been wounded for the seventh time.

11
00:00:42,400 --> 00:00:47,420
He never returned to the front and trained tank NCOs until the end of the war.

12
00:00:47,440 --> 00:00:55,940
He is credited with about 139 tanks destroyed; figures range from 89 to 144.

13
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:00,360
Johannes Bölter died in 1987 at the age of 72.
